    "lawCode": "Amd §§2590-b, 2590-f, 2590-g, 2590-h, 2590-i, 2590-q, 2590-r, 402-a & 2590-c, Ed L; amd §§93, 259 & 260, add §530, NYC Chart; amd §34, Chap 91 of 2002",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5576\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the education law and the New York city charter, in\nrelation to enacting the \"better schools act\"; expanding the duties of\nthe city board and other officials of the city of New York, and the\nindependent budget office; establishing procedures and policies for\ncertain education related matters, and creating the center for parent\nand student service and empowerment\n\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\nThis bill would amend the education law and the New York city charter,\nin relation to enacting the \"better schools act\"; expanding the duties\nof the city board and other officials of the city of New York, and the\nindependent budget office; establishing procedures and policies for\ncertain education related matters, and creating the center for parent\nand student services.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\nThe Panel for Education Policy (PEP) needs a degree of independence in\norder to promote adequate public debate and add some balance in\ndecision-making procedures.\n\nThe key provisions which accomplish the Legislature's goal are as\nfollows:\n\nSection 2. This section sets forth the Legislature's goals of\nstrengthening decision-making, promoting and emphasizing open\ncommunications and public forums, and engaging more shared\ndecision-making by and between the stakeholder groups.\n\nSection 3. This section provides the substantive changes to the Panel\non Education Policy. The PEP will more effectively represent the\ndiverse needs of the city, with each member having fixed terms of\noffice of two years in duration. The PEP will be expanded to 17\nmembers, who will be appointed as follows:\n\n* 8 appointed by the Mayor\n* 1 by each of the borough presidents\n* 1 by the city council\n* 1 by the governor\n* 1 by the Speaker of the Assembly\n* 1 by the Temporary President of the Senate\n\nThe bill empowers the PEP to vote on the selection of a Chairperson of\nthe PEP from among its voting members.\n\nSection 4: The District Superintendents for the 32 Community School\nDistricts shall be empowered to supervise and evaluate principals of\nthe schools within their school districts and to address issues of\nschool choice, student discipline, language access for parents and\nstudents, the education of students and with disabilities, and shared\ndecision making within those schools.\n\nSection 5: The Chancellor is responsible for proposing the DOE\noperating budget, the five-year capital plan, procurement contracts,\nand changes to educational policies and standards and the PEP has the\nresponsibility to vote on the approval of those items proposed by the\nChancellor.\n\nSection 6: Procurement contracts that exceed $200,000 would require\nBoard approval.\n\nSection 7: This Legislation requires that the Chancellor be appointed\nby the Mayor and serve as an ex officio member of the PEP.\nSection 12: The Community Education Council's role with regards to\nschool closings, resitings and insertions shall he clarified and\nstrengthened to ensure adequate local role in decision making\nregarding school closings, resitings and insertions.\n\nSection 12-a. The Comptroller of the City of New York shall have the\npower to audit the Department of Education.\n\nSection 14: The Independent Budget Office shall be required to produce\nreports on Department of Education finances and academic achievement\nand shall he provided full access to all necessary records to perform\nthis function.\n\nSection 15: This legislation requires meaningful Parent, Student and\nCommunity Participation in Department of Education decisions, by\ncreating a Center for Parent and Student Service and Empowerment under\nthe office of the Public Advocate, with the charge of outreach,\ntraining and support for parents and students.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThe reforms to New York City school governance enacted in 2002 and\n2003 expire in 2009. The legislature should not allow these crucial\nreforms to lapse, as this would return school governance to the prior\noutdated system that no longer exists. However, the current system can\nand must he improved.\n\nThis legislation has three core areas of focus: checks and balances,\ntransparency and accountability, and public participation. The reform\nof mayoral control of school governance must take reasonable and\nmeaningful steps to address the need to improve school governance in\nall three areas.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\nNone. New Bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\nMinimal.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
